\end{table}

Among the general baselines only the proposed method applies the admissibility rule, so the whole-system comparison under misaligned evidence does not isolate it; the fixed rule with admissibility and the corresponding ablation do. Budget-compliant methods never exceeded a slot budget in any run and provenance completeness was 100\% for every method and condition.

\section{Confirmatory Results on the Synthetic Benchmark}
\label{sec:results}
\subsection{When Bandwidth Is the Only Constraint, a Fixed Rule Is Enough}
Table~\ref{tab:lowbw} gives the low-bandwidth condition. Among budget-compliant methods the policy and the fixed rule made statistically indistinguishable numbers of errors, a paired difference of $-0.8$ $[-1.7, 0.2]$. At five seeds the fixed rule held a nominal advantage of $+0.2$ errors; at twenty the nominal sign is the other way and the interval still spans zero, so what the wider run supports is that neither method is ahead here rather than that the simple rule wins. The policy spent 1,745 more bytes per window $[1{,}732, 1{,}757]$ and added 0.33~s to tail latency $[0.330, 0.334]$, buying image features that do not change the decision under this byte model. Transmit-all reaches 84.6~s at the tail because six windows share one link. The same pattern holds wherever the byte budget is the only binding constraint: under normal connectivity, under an intermittent outage and with imagery missing, the paired difference spans zero in each case. It does not hold once the evidence itself degrades. With text missing the policy is ahead by 2.75 errors $[1.03, 4.47]$, under noisy text by 1.30 $[0.13, 2.47]$ and under contradictory evidence by 2.50 $[0.80, 4.20]$, all three excluding zero at twenty seeds where five seeds could not separate them (Table~\ref{tab:allcond}). When no decision involves waiting, admissibility or degraded evidence, the optimizer adds cost rather than value. Section~\ref{sec:link} shows that the cost grows once the link is modelled with loss and measured variability.

\begin{table}[t]
