\end{table}

\subsection{Waiting for Late Evidence: The Threshold Matters, the Solver Does Not}
\label{sec:delay}
Table~\ref{tab:delay} gives the delayed-imagery condition. Methods that always wait paid a median latency near 682.8~s. The policy waited for 37\% of images, those with short forecast delays or uncertain partial evidence as (\ref{eq:threshold}) predicts, holding its median latency at 0.19~s. Against the wait-if-uncertain rule, which ignores the forecast, it changed tail latency by $-1{,}197$~s $[-1{,}250, -1{,}145]$. Removing the latency penalty cost $9.1$ errors $[5.6, 12.5]$ and made it wait for every image, which confirms that the term is doing the work (Table~\ref{tab:ablation}).

\begin{table}[t]
\caption{Delayed imagery. Errors out of 720 with 95\% intervals, mean of twenty seeds; median and 95th-percentile modelled latency; share of images sent. *Ignores the budget.}
\label{tab:delay}
\centering\scriptsize\setlength{\tabcolsep}{3pt}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrrrr@{}}
\toprule
Method & Errors [95\% CI] & Median s & p95 s & Image sent \\
\midrule
Image only & 71.5 [59.1, 83.9] & 682.80 & 1,864.4 & 100\% \\
Text only & 75.7 [63.9, 87.4] & 0.15 & 0.1 & 0\% \\
Transmit all* & 19.2 [15.5, 23.0] & 688.69 & 1,869.5 & 100\% \\
Confidence gate & 25.6 [21.1, 30.0] & 682.81 & 1,864.4 & 100\% \\
Fixed compression & 18.9 [14.9, 23.0] & 682.84 & 1,864.4 & 100\% \\
Fixed rule & 23.4 [18.7, 28.1] & 682.82 & 1,864.4 & 100\% \\
Random within budget & 32.1 [28.1, 36.2] & 39.69 & 1,664.7 & 68\% \\
Wait if uncertain & 16.0 [12.3, 19.7] & 42.56 & 1,562.3 & 53\% \\
Derived threshold $T^\ast$ & 10.8 [8.6, 13.0] & 0.18 & 25.7 & 31\% \\
Proposed, $\lambda_\ell=0.0004$ & 10.2 [8.3, 12.0] & 0.19 & 364.9 & 37\% \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The latency reduction does not require the optimizer. The derived-threshold rule sends the fixed rule's image summary whenever the forecast delay falls below the calibration-derived constant $T^\ast$. It waited for 31\% of images and made 10.8 errors at 25.7~s tail latency. Relative to it the policy changed errors by $-0.7$ $[-1.5, 0.2]$, tail latency by $+339.2$~s $[314.4, 363.9]$ and bytes by $+688$ $[670, 706]$. The error interval includes zero at twenty seeds as it did at five, which is the evidence for the claim in this subsection title. The policy's longer tail comes from per-window adaptivity: it waited more than 300~s for 5.9\% of windows against 0.5\% for the constant rule, when uncertain partial evidence raised the value of a late image. Those long waits bought nothing here. Fig.~\ref{fig:frontier} shows that the constant-threshold frontier lies on or slightly below the policy's throughout.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig_delay_frontier.png}
\caption{Delayed imagery: errors against 95th-percentile modelled latency. The solid line traces the policy across latency-penalty values; the dashed line traces the constant-threshold rule across $T$. The star marks $T^\ast$ derived from calibration events, not selected on test data.}
\label{fig:frontier}
\end{figure}

The accuracy comparison carries a caveat that changes the conclusion. The policy changed errors by $-13.3$ $[-17.3, -9.2]$ against the fixed rule and by $-5.9$ $[-8.5, -3.2]$ against wait-if-uncertain. Those gains exist because the benchmark decays the weight of text and sensor evidence while a decision waits. With that decay removed, the always-wait baselines improve by 4.8 to 5.8 errors while the two forecast-using rules change by at most 0.6, so the ordering reverses: the fixed rule reaches 5.2 errors and wait-if-uncertain 5.4 against 9.7 for the policy and 10.7 for the derived threshold (sensitivity rows of the twenty-seed ablation results). Without staleness, both forecast-using rules trade four to five errors for their latency reduction. Whether acting early also improves accuracy therefore depends on how fast real evidence goes stale, which this benchmark assumes rather than measures. Section~\ref{sec:realthreshold} tests exactly that on a corpus with no decay, where the trade is the one this sensitivity predicts.

\subsection{Admissibility, Not Optimization, Keeps Foreign Reports Out}
\label{sec:adm}
When 30\% of text items came from other events, methods without the admissibility rule fused all of them. The proposed method fused 24\% of misaligned items, exactly those whose metadata had been made compatible and rejected no aligned item. The complete system made 13.9 errors against 35.0 for the fixed rule without admissibility, a paired difference of $-21.1$ $[-26.2, -15.9]$. The fixed rule with the same filter made 15.3 errors; relative to it the proposed method changed errors by $-1.5$ $[-2.7, -0.3]$. The filter accounts for almost all of the gain and at twenty seeds the residual advantage of the optimizer over the filtered simple rule is small while no longer spanning zero. Within the proposed system, disabling admissibility added $+16.2$ errors $[11.1, 21.3]$; at five seeds that interval included zero, so the filter's own effect is now estimated precisely enough to read.

This test favours the filter by construction. Aligned evidence shares the window's cell and hazard tag exactly and is at most 60~s old, far inside the tolerances, while misaligned reports that were not spoofed carry the coordinates of another event on a $400\times400$ grid. Zero false rejections follow from the generator, not from the rule's robustness. Real metadata carries positional error, clock skew and missing geotags, none of which is represented here.

\subsection{Conformal Coverage Does Not Survive a Change of Condition}
\label{sec:coverage}
Calibrating per condition kept empirical coverage between 0.883 and 0.898 against a nominal 0.90; the shortfall is consistent with having only two calibration events. Reusing the normal-condition threshold is where the trouble starts. Coverage falls to 0.794 under contradictory evidence and to 0.745 with images missing, a loss of 13.8 percentage points in the condition where an operator is most likely to rely on the interval. Proposition~2 should not be stretched across conditions: a deployment that calibrates once at commissioning will be miscalibrated exactly when the network degrades. Section~\ref{sec:real} shows that per-condition recalibration is necessary but not sufficient, because on real data the guarantee also fails across events.

\subsection{The Review Queue Is the Real Operational Cost}
\label{sec:queue}
Across all nine conditions, about 30\% of windows entered the combined abstention and review queue under low bandwidth, roughly 61 queued windows per caught error and 53\% under contradictory evidence (the full sensitivity table is in the package). Under low bandwidth 98\% of queued windows were correctly classified, so almost all reviewer effort confirmed a decision the system had already got right. Conformal abstention alone queued about 10\% of windows and still caught every error; the prespecified conflict rule nearly tripled the queue without catching an additional one. Across conditions the conflict rule added 11 to 43 percentage points of queue load for between zero and two additional caught errors per 720 windows. We keep the prespecified settings rather than tune after the fact; the implication is that the conflict threshold is a staffing decision and should be set from reviewer capacity. The severity-based escalation rule was nearly inert, firing on 0.07\% of windows against the 28\% the generating severity would have escalated, because the severity estimator is weak.

\subsection{When the Shared Budget Binds (Pre-registered)}
\label{sec:contested}
None of the conditions above makes the shared budget bind, which is the only regime in which the knapsack can differ from a per-window rule. At the reference difficulty it could not have mattered: on calibration events, sending only summaries erred on 0.56\% of windows against 0.44\% for the unconstrained fixed rule. We therefore froze a protocol before running the proposed method on a harder condition, with the protocol file and its SHA-256 digest inside the package. Observation noise was scaled by 1.75, the smallest value in a pre-specified grid at which summaries alone err on at least 8\% of calibration windows. Models were retrained at that difficulty, the link runs at 256~kbps with $K=6$ and per-window budgets range from 700 to 1,900 bytes, with ten seeds. The pre-specified competitor is a shared-budget greedy rule that starts from summaries and upgrades items across the slot in order of local uncertainty; a greedy heuristic on the proposed utility isolates the exact solver. A difference counts only if its paired $t$-test survives Holm adjustment across the five budgets at 0.05.

\begin{table}[t]
\caption{Contested shared budget (noise scale 1.75, ten seeds): errors out of 720 by per-window byte budget $B$. *Ignores the budget; shown as a ceiling.}
\label{tab:contested}
\centering\scriptsize\setlength{\tabcolsep}{3pt}
\begin{tabular}{@{}p{2.3cm}rrrrr@{}}
\toprule
Method & 700 & 900 & 1,200 & 1,500 & 1,900 \\
\midrule
Summaries only & 78.6 & 78.7 & 78.6 & 78.6 & 78.8 \\
Fixed rule, per window & 78.6 & 75.1 & 68.9 & 65.6 & 64.0 \\
Confidence gate & 78.6 & 76.0 & 70.0 & 66.3 & 64.7 \\
Random within budget & 140.4 & 141.3 & 127.1 & 121.1 & 109.2 \\
Greedy by uncertainty & 76.0 & 71.2 & 68.9 & 63.3 & 61.9 \\
Greedy, proposed utility & 96.4 & 72.3 & 64.6 & 60.7 & 57.2 \\
Proposed (exact knapsack) & 96.8 & 72.6 & 64.9 & 59.7 & 57.1 \\
Fixed rule, ignores budget* & 57.6 & 57.5 & 57.7 & 57.5 & 57.4 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The optimizer did not beat the pre-specified competitor at any budget (Table~\ref{tab:contested}, Fig.~\ref{fig:contested}). At 700 bytes it was worse by $+20.8$ errors $[15.4, 26.2]$ with Holm $p<0.001$. At the three largest budgets it led by $-4.0$, $-3.6$ and $-4.8$ errors and none survived adjustment (Holm $p$ 0.075 to 0.219). The exact solver matched a greedy solver on the same utility to within one error at every budget. One of those five differences, at $B=1{,}500$ bytes, survived Holm adjustment in the solver's favour ($p=0.042$); it is 1.0 error in 720 (0.14\% of windows). We do not regard that as a benefit worth an exact solver, but the pre-specified decision rule was met once and it would be wrong to say the solver never beat greedy.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig_contested.png}
\caption{Contested shared budget. (a) Errors against the per-window budget; marginal intervals are omitted because between-seed variation dominates them. The pale diamonds are the greedy heuristic on the proposed utility, which tracks the exact knapsack closely enough to be read as a halo around it. Random-within-budget is omitted because it runs from 140.4 to 109.2 errors; it is in Table~\ref{tab:contested}. (b) The paired difference the analysis tests, with 95\% intervals over ten seeds. Only the tight-budget failure survives Holm adjustment.}
\label{fig:contested}
\end{figure}

The tight-budget failure has a clean mechanism. At 700 bytes the optimizer sent nothing for the image in 27\% of windows and nothing for the text in 30\% and held at least one modality in 47\%, in order to buy raw sensor records elsewhere in the slot. It did so because the value function prices a held modality at zero rather than at the cost of losing it. Exact optimization does not repair a misspecified objective; it exploits it more efficiently than a greedy rule can.

A variant designed after these results adds a summary floor: every eligible item is sent at least as a summary and the knapsack chooses only upgrades. We froze a second protocol and tested it on ten new seeds. The floor removed the tight-budget failure. Its advantage over greedy-by-uncertainty replicated in direction at the three largest budgets ($-3.0$, $-3.1$ and $-3.4$ errors) but did not survive adjustment (Holm $p$ 0.087 to 0.353) and was absent at the two tightest budgets ($+0.2$ and $-0.9$). The exact solver again matched a greedy solver with the same floor to within one error. The proposed value function may carry a benefit of roughly 0.4\% of windows at moderate budgets; this benchmark does not establish it and the exact solver contributes nothing measurable.

\section{Exploratory Analyses}
\label{sec:exploratory}
The experiments in this section were designed after the confirmatory results and are reported as robustness analyses, not as hypothesis tests. Each inherits the perturbation stream of the condition it modifies, so it is a controlled comparison rather than a differently drawn workload; we verified that at zero loss, with no trace and with one controller, every method's errors, bytes and latencies equal the earlier values to every printed digit.

\subsection{Adversarial Metadata: The Filter Is Exactly as Strong as Forgery Is Hard}
\label{sec:forgery}
The admissibility rule reads metadata and nothing else, so an adversary who can attach plausible location, time and hazard values to a report defeats it by construction. We hold the misaligned-evidence condition fixed and vary the fraction of misaligned reports whose metadata is set to the target incident's own values. Table~\ref{tab:forgery} shows an almost linear collapse. With no forgery the filter is worth 7.0 errors against 20.2 for the unfiltered rule. With complete forgery it is worth nothing: 20.2 against 20.2. The admitted fraction tracks the forged fraction almost exactly (24.1\%, 48.4\% and 72.4\% against 25\%, 50\% and 75\%), so the rule admits the forged items and nothing else and rejects no aligned item at any point. The optimizer contributes nothing to robustness: the proposed method tracks the filtered fixed rule within 0.6 to 3.0 errors across the range.

\begin{table}[t]
\caption{Errors out of 720 as the share of misaligned reports carrying forged metadata rises, with an uncompromised text source (left) and a compromised source corrupting 35\% of reports (right). Means of five seeds. The 25\% column with an uncompromised source is the reference condition of Section~\ref{sec:adm} and reproduces it exactly.}
\label{tab:forgery}
\centering\scriptsize
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{2.5pt}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrrrrrrrrr@{}}
\toprule
 & \multicolumn{5}{c}{Uncompromised source} & \multicolumn{4}{c}{Compromised source} \\
\cmidrule(lr){2-6}\cmidrule(lr){7-10}
Method & 0\% & 25\% & 50\% & 75\% & 100\% & 0\% & 25\% & 50\% & 100\% \\
\midrule
Fixed rule (no filter) & 20.2 & 20.2 & 20.2 & 20.2 & 20.2 & 19.8 & 19.8 & 19.8 & 19.8 \\
Fixed rule + adm.\ & 7.0 & 9.4 & 11.4 & 15.0 & 20.2 & 10.6 & 14.0 & 16.0 & 19.8 \\
Proposed & 6.4 & 8.8 & 10.2 & 12.8 & 17.2 & 10.2 & 12.8 & 14.8 & 18.4 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Two consequences follow. There is no safe operating point, so a deployment cannot argue that a low expected forgery rate makes the rule sufficient. And metadata admissibility is a cheap filter against accident (misrouted reports, stale caches, misconfigured clients), not a security control; where an adversary is in the threat model it must be backed by signed observations or attested sources, whose cost belongs in the byte budget. We have not evaluated such a scheme and the sweep models metadata substitution only. Crossing the forgery rate with a compromised text source that points 35\% of reports toward a wrong class (right half of Table~\ref{tab:forgery}) shows that forgery alone is sufficient: with both capabilities the filtered rule reaches 19.8 errors, no worse than forgery alone.

\subsection{Packet Loss and a Measured Link}
\label{sec:link}
The results so far treat the link as a constant-rate pipe. Two experiments relax that assumption. The first splits each payload into 1,500-byte packets, each lost independently with probability $p$ and retransmitted, so the bytes placed on the wire grow by $1/(1-p)$ and each lost packet costs one 200~ms retransmission timeout. The expected number of lost packets grows with the payload, so the time penalty is super-linear in bytes. Accuracy is unmoved, because nothing in this condition ties latency back to the decision; tail latency is not (Table~\ref{tab:loss}). The fixed rule goes from 0.09~s to 0.14~s at 10\% loss, a factor of 1.5; the proposed policy goes from 0.57~s to 4.70~s, a factor of 8.3. The gap between them widens from 6$\times$ to 34$\times$. The optimizer's surplus bytes were close to free under an idealised link; under a lossy one they are the dominant term.

\begin{table}[t]
\caption{95th-percentile modelled latency in seconds against packet loss, at the normal per-window budget. *Ignores the budget.}
\label{tab:loss}
\centering\footnotesize
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrrrrr@{}}
\toprule
Method & 0\% & 1\% & 2\% & 5\% & 10\% \\
\midrule
Fixed rule & 0.09 & 0.10 & 0.10 & 0.12 & 0.14 \\
Confidence gate & 0.33 & 0.39 & 0.40 & 0.41 & 0.44 \\
Fixed compression & 0.10 & 0.11 & 0.12 & 0.14 & 0.17 \\
Proposed & 0.57 & 0.83 & 1.23 & 2.47 & 4.70 \\
Transmit all* & 2.24 & 2.89 & 3.64 & 6.12 & 10.72 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The second experiment replays measured cellular capacity traces in place of the constant rate~\cite{mahimahi}. Each trace records the milliseconds at which one MTU packet could be delivered on a real LTE uplink; we replay them unmodified, varying only the starting offset by slot and compare against a constant link at the same mean capacity, which isolates variability from speed (Table~\ref{tab:trace}). Holding mean capacity fixed, real variability inflates tail latency by up to 8.7$\times$. On the Verizon driving trace, whose fifth-percentile throughput falls to 0.25~Mbps, the proposed policy's tail rises from 0.25~s to 2.17~s and the fixed rule's from 0.24~s to 1.49~s; the steadier traces inflate far less, so the penalty tracks the depth of the fades, not the average rate. Ranking the five methods by tail latency gives the same order under the replayed trace as under its mean-matched constant for all three traces, so the ordering survives the more realistic link; what does not survive is the idea that the optimizer's extra bytes are cheap.

