\subsection{Coordination Is Worth More to the Optimizer Than to the Simple Rule}
\label{sec:coord}
Every result so far grants the shared-budget formulation its most favourable assumption: one controller owns all six windows in a slot. An incident area is more plausibly served by several edge nodes that cannot coordinate per slot over the degraded link they are conserving. We deal the slot's windows round-robin to $C$ independent controllers, each allocating only its own windows against a share of the byte budget proportional to the windows it owns. The total budget is unchanged and all controllers still share the physical link. The contested-budget condition is used because it is the only regime in which the shared budget binds; $C=1$ reproduces Table~\ref{tab:contested} exactly.

\begin{table}[t]
\caption{Errors out of 720 at $B=900$ bytes as coordination is removed. $C$ is the number of independent controllers sharing one slot. Ten seeds.}
\label{tab:coord}
\centering\footnotesize
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrrrr@{}}
\toprule
Method & $C=1$ & $C=2$ & $C=3$ & $C=6$ \\
\midrule
Summaries only & 78.7 & 78.7 & 78.7 & 78.7 \\
Fixed rule, per window & 75.1 & 75.1 & 75.1 & 75.1 \\
Greedy by uncertainty & 71.2 & 71.4 & 72.0 & 75.1 \\
Proposed (exact knapsack) & 72.6 & 78.3 & 81.5 & 92.6 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The two per-window methods are controls: they allocate nothing across windows, so $C$ cannot affect them and it does not (Table~\ref{tab:coord}). The two coordinated methods separate sharply. Removing coordination costs the exact knapsack $+20.0$ errors $[14.2, 25.8]$ at $B=900$ and the greedy uncertainty ranking only $+3.9$ $[0.8, 7.0]$. The penalty peaks at $B=900$ rather than at $B=700$ ($+10.2$), because at 700 bytes the centralised optimizer already fails badly enough that little is left to take away. At $C=6$ the proposed method makes 92.6 errors against the greedy rule's 75.1, so a rough tie under centralisation becomes a clear loss once the assumption is dropped. The exact optimizer is not merely no better than a greedy ranking; it is more dependent on an architectural assumption that field deployments are least likely to satisfy and this benchmark charges nothing for the per-slot consensus it would need.

\subsection{What the Policy Costs to Compute}
\label{sec:compute}
We timed the allocation step on the contested-budget views as best-of-five wall clock on one workstation and recorded peak allocation (Table~\ref{tab:compute}); no energy figure is inferred. The fixed rule decides a slot in 0.0012~ms; the exact knapsack takes 7.44~ms, about 6,190 times longer and holds 18 times the peak memory. Solve time grows close to linearly in the number of windows sharing a slot (1: 0.10~ms, 2: 0.42, 3: 0.88, 4: 1.44, 6: 2.37, 8: 3.24, 12: 4.83~ms), as the $O(K\cdot64\cdot2000)$ bound predicts. The greedy heuristic on the proposed utility is slower than the exact program (15.5 against 7.4~ms) only because it is a Python loop while the dynamic program is vectorised.

