\section{A Reduced Comparison on Real Disaster Data}
\label{sec:real}
Every result above rests on a generated workload; the only measured input so far is the capacity traces. This section replaces the synthetic workload with one built from CrisisMMD~\cite{crisismmd}: 3,526 labelled report--image pairs from seven disasters in 2017, with real text, photographs, hazard types, arrival times recovered from the message identifiers, class imbalance and payload sizes. The policies are unchanged and the split is by event. The experiment was designed after the synthetic results, was not pre-registered and is exploratory.

\subsection{Representations and Their Diagnostic}
\label{sec:repr}
Both modalities are represented by CLIP embeddings~\cite{clip}, reduced by principal components fitted on training events only: eight components for text and ten for images, matching the dimensions the policy expects. Measured on the 968 held-out records before any budgeting decision, the image representation reaches 0.566 accuracy against a majority baseline of 0.490, while the text representation returns 0.490, the majority baseline to four decimal places: the text classifier predicts the majority class for every held-out record. A TF--IDF text representation tested before the CLIP encoder also returned the baseline. These are raw per-record accuracies, not the per-window error counts decided under a budget.

The first submission could not say whether that majority-class outcome was a property of the task, of the eight-component reduction or of class imbalance. A factor diagnostic now separates them. Twenty-five cells were scored on the rotation-0 split, each over five fitting seeds, crossing five text representations (the eight shipped components, thirty-two components, the full 512-dimension CLIP embedding, a TF--IDF control and the deployed leading-four truncation), three class handlings (none, balanced class weights, balanced resampling of the training rows) and three classifiers (the package edge model, logistic regression as a floor and the deployed truncation). The decision rule was fixed before the run: the pathway counts as informative under some tested representation only if the best cell clears the majority baseline by more than 0.02 accuracy and exceeds the majority classifier's macro-F1 in at least five of the seven rotations.

The rule returns false, in none of the seven rotations. The best cell, the full CLIP embedding with balanced resampling, raises macro-F1 from the majority classifier's 0.219 to 0.452 on rotation 0 and its macro-F1 gain is positive in all seven rotations, yet its accuracy sits at 0.481 against a majority baseline of 0.490 and is below that baseline in every rotation. Three cells do clear the accuracy bar on rotation 0, by 0.028 to 0.046, all of them without class handling; none survives the rotations, passing in two, two and one of seven. So the answer is not that a better representation unlocks the pathway. What class handling recovers is macro-F1 rather than accuracy: the pathway carries class-discriminative information that an accuracy comparison on imbalanced classes hides while still not predicting this label well enough to clear a bar set in advance. The claim this supports is therefore the original one with a sharper reason behind it: on this corpus the text pathway contributes no predictive signal that survives the pre-registered test, the cause is class imbalance rather than the dimensionality of the reduction and nothing in the value function detects either.

\subsection{What Carries Over}
The comparison is reduced and the reductions are forced by the data. CrisisMMD has no sensor stream, so the workload has two modalities and the per-window action space shrinks from 64 combinations to 16; a fixed-seed placeholder keeps the shared code path running and the runner asserts it is never available. The annotations carry no geolocation, so the spatial term of (\ref{eq:adm}) reduces to event identity. No severity field exists that is independent of the damage label, so severity is held constant and its term in the value function is inert. Imagery is never missing, so the incomplete-modality machinery is not exercised. Three conditions carry over, not nine. Three methods are absent, including the fixed rule with admissibility, so the misaligned condition here cannot isolate the filter as Section~\ref{sec:adm} does. CrisisMMD carries no delayed imagery of its own, so the derived constant wait threshold, the clearest positive result of Section~\ref{sec:results}, was untested on real data in the first submission. It is now tested on a constructed delay: for each window an image arrival delay is drawn from the same log-normal family as the synthetic condition, with the seed and the draw recorded in a protocol frozen before the run and its digest written into the run manifest. The delay is therefore declared rather than observed, which is the honest description of what the corpus allows, while every other quantity in the test stays real. Section~\ref{sec:realthreshold} reports the outcome.

Five seeds vary model fitting only; the data, the split and the 968 test windows are identical across them and the test set is two disasters (795 windows from Hurricane Irma, 173 from the Iraq--Iran earthquake). Intervals here bound fitting jitter rather than sampling error; several are zero-width because the method is deterministic given the data. The five are the package seeds, the same five every published number in this section averages; the reruns reported below reproduce all ten methods of Table~\ref{tab:real} exactly before any new column is interpreted.

