\subsection{Results}
\label{sec:realresults}
Against the fixed rule the ranking does not carry over (Table~\ref{tab:real}). The optimizer wins under normal connectivity by 17.2 errors $[15.8, 18.6]$ and under misaligned evidence by 11.2 $[7.8, 14.6]$ and loses only under low bandwidth, by 1.4 $[0.7, 2.1]$, which is 0.14\% of the test set. Reporting only the low-bandwidth column would have reproduced the synthetic conclusion by selection. One assignment of seven events to three splits produced that pattern and the test set is dominated by one hurricane, so we rotated the event list through seven pre-specified assignments, each keeping the same 3/2/2 shape, so that every event serves in the test set at least once (Table~\ref{tab:rotation}). The rotations were fixed before running and none was discarded. The direction survives the rotation and the per-condition pattern does not. The optimizer is ahead of the fixed rule in 15 of the 21 comparisons and under normal connectivity in every one of the seven, but the loss under low bandwidth occurs in 2 rotations of 7 and the win under misaligned evidence in 3. What generalises from this corpus is therefore narrower than Table~\ref{tab:real} alone suggests: on real data the optimizer is ahead of the fixed rule more often than not, decisively so when the link is not the constraint and which way any single condition falls depends on which disasters land in the test set.

