\caption{Proposed minus fixed rule in errors, by event split. Negative means the optimizer wins. Rotation 0 is the split of Table~\ref{tab:real}. Counts differ because the events differ in size.}
\label{tab:rotation}
\centering\scriptsize\setlength{\tabcolsep}{3pt}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lp{2.05cm}rrrr@{}}
\toprule
Rot. & Test events & $n$ & Normal & Low bw. & Misaligned \\
\midrule
0 & Irma + Iraq--Iran & 968 & $-17.2$ & $+1.4$ & $-11.2$ \\
1 & Harvey + Iraq--Iran & 1,065 & $-3.0$ & $-5.4$ & $+2.8$ \\
2 & Harvey + Maria & 1,754 & $-0.2$ & $+1.0$ & $+1.6$ \\
3 & California + Maria & 1,393 & $-18.8$ & $-12.8$ & $+8.2$ \\
4 & California + Mexico & 709 & $-3.0$ & $-1.0$ & $+18.8$ \\
5 & Mexico + Sri Lanka & 273 & $-7.6$ & $-8.6$ & $-6.4$ \\
6 & Irma + Sri Lanka & 890 & $-57.2$ & $-33.2$ & $-47.6$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The comparison that matters most for this paper is between the exact solver and a greedy heuristic on the same utility and on real data the two are identical: the same errors, bytes per window and 95th-percentile latency in all three conditions and at every seed, a paired difference of exactly 0.0. A greedy ranking by local uncertainty, which does not use the utility, makes 466.8 errors under normal connectivity against 447.0, so the utility is doing work even though the solver is not (Fig.~\ref{fig:real}).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig_real_scatter.png}
\caption{Errors against bytes on real data under low bandwidth. Intervals are fitting jitter across five seeds on one fixed test set and are zero-width for the deterministic methods. The exact knapsack and the greedy heuristic over the same utility occupy the same point.}
\label{fig:real}
\end{figure}

What beats everything on errors is image-only, at 420.0 in every condition, ahead of the optimizer by 46.8 errors $[46.2, 47.4]$ under low bandwidth while sending 224 bytes. Its value cannot vary because the conditions perturb text and bandwidth and image-only consumes neither, so its constancy is not evidence of robustness; on the synthetic benchmark the same method makes 51.8 errors against the fixed rule's 4.2, so a method that is near-worst on one workload and best on the other is telling us about the workloads. The reason it wins is visible in the same table: text-only scores the majority-class count, 494.0, in every condition and everything that fuses text pays for it. Severity, reliability, recency, fidelity, uncertainty, quality and disagreement are all properties of the source or the signal; none asks whether this modality has ever predicted this label. A held-out estimate of predictive contribution is the quantity that would have been needed. Transmit-all is also ahead of the optimizer on errors, by 31.2 $[30.2, 32.2]$, but sends 129,728 bytes against 1,896 and pays 30.7~s of tail latency against 0.63~s.

Two assumptions do not survive the move. Conformal coverage, which held between 0.883 and 0.898 across the synthetic conditions, falls to 0.816 under normal connectivity and 0.745 under low bandwidth against a nominal 0.90. Calibration and test windows come from different disasters, so the exchangeability that Proposition~2 assumes fails within a condition; per-condition recalibration cannot repair that and the 7.1-point spread across the three conditions shows a condition-dependent component this explanation does not account for. The byte model of Section~\ref{sec:workload} is contradicted by measurement: real images have a median of 103,142.5 bytes against an assumed 220,000 to 620,000 and every report is smaller than the smallest summary the method defines, 39 to 181 bytes against an assumed 350 to 1,800. Sending a report verbatim is cheaper than summarising it, so the summarise-or-send decision is vacuous for that modality on this corpus. Byte sizes are measurable before a budget is designed around them.

\subsection{The Constant Threshold on Real Data}
\label{sec:realthreshold}
The derived constant threshold is the clearest positive result of the synthetic benchmark, so it is the one worth testing hardest. CrisisMMD carries no late imagery, so the arrival delay is constructed from the same log-normal family as the synthetic condition, drawn once from a protocol frozen before the run whose digest is in the run manifest; every other quantity stays as the corpus supplies it. $T^\ast$ is computed on calibration events only, as (\ref{eq:tstar}) defines it, landing between 164.0 and 186.8~s across the rotations.

The latency half of the synthetic result carries over without qualification and the
accuracy half reverses. On rotation 0 the threshold rule holds 95th-percentile latency at
25.4~s where the always-wait rules reach 1,833.9~s, a factor of 72, in all seven rotations: 25.1 to 26.8~s against 1,739.2 to 1,914.8~s. It pays for that with
errors, 486.0 against 451.0 out of 968. The decision rule for this test was fixed in
advance and asks for both halves: the threshold rule's tail latency below
wait-if-uncertain's in all seven rotations, which holds, together with its error count inside the
proposed policy's paired interval in at least five, which holds in one. The rule therefore
returns \emph{does not carry over}. The reason is specific rather than general. Acting
early improved accuracy on the synthetic benchmark only because that benchmark decays the
weight of evidence while a decision waits, as Section~\ref{sec:delay} already reports. The
real corpus has no such decay, so waiting costs nothing in evidence quality and the
latency saved is bought with accuracy rather than alongside it.

One observation bears on baseline selection. The fixed rule and wait-if-uncertain are indistinguishable here at every seed and rotation because wait-if-uncertain's branch, text and sensor confidence below 0.90, fires on every window of this corpus, so the baseline meant to ignore the forecast collapses into the one that always waits. A baseline whose branch never fires is not a comparison.

\subsection{Class Imbalance in the Budgeting Pipeline}
\label{sec:realimbalance}
Section~\ref{sec:repr} attributes the text pathway's behaviour to class imbalance, so the question is what the budgeting comparison does when the imbalance is handled everywhere rather than only diagnosed. Balanced resampling, the handling the diagnostic selected, was applied to every per-modality edge and cloud model before the comparison was rerun. The unhandled column reproduces all ten methods of Table~\ref{tab:real} exactly, which is the precondition for reading the handled column.

The direction of the headline claim survives and its magnitude does not. The optimizer
stays ahead of the fixed rule in 14 of the 21 comparisons against 15 without handling, together with all seven rotations under normal connectivity in both columns. The sizes move far more
than that count suggests: under normal connectivity on rotation 0 the gap widens from
$-17.2$ errors to $-112.6$. Methods also move in opposite directions. Everything that
fuses two modalities improves, the optimizer from 447.0 to 385.6 errors and transmit-all
from 435.8 to 377.8, while every single-modality rule gets worse, text-only from 494.0 to
576.4 and image-only from 420.0 to 466.6. Handling the imbalance makes the fused
prediction better and the single-modality prediction worse, which is consistent with
resampling trading majority-class accuracy for minority-class recall. The comparison is
thus sensitive to class handling in size while not in sign. The single-modality rule
that beats everything in Table~\ref{tab:real} does so partly because the imbalance was
left in place.

\subsection{A Label the Paper Did Not Choose}
\label{sec:reallabel}
Every real-data number above scores the damage label annotated from the photograph, which is this paper's own choice, so the sharpest available test is to replace it with one the dataset's annotators defined. CrisisMMD labels every record with a humanitarian category independently of the damage field. The decision machinery is fixed at three classes, so the task is restricted to the three most frequent, keeping 2,763 of 3,526 records: other relevant information, infrastructure and utility damage, plus rescue, volunteering or donation effort. That restriction is a property of the machinery rather than a selection made after seeing results. Table~\ref{tab:extlabel} reports the five discarded categories. This is not the published eight-category task and no published baseline is reproduced here; Section~\ref{sec:limits} says why neither was possible.

This is where the comparison fails. On the annotators' label the optimizer is ahead of the
fixed rule in 11 of the 21 comparisons rather than 15, together with 3 of the 7 rotations under
normal connectivity rather than all 7. With the class handling of
Section~\ref{sec:realimbalance} applied as well it is ahead in 8 of 21. The claim in
Section~\ref{sec:realresults} that survives the event rotation, that the optimizer is
ahead under normal connectivity in every one of the seven, does not survive a change of
label on the same records, the same splits and the same conditions. What generalises from
this corpus is therefore narrower again than the rotation analysis alone establishes: the
direction of the real-data result is a property of the label as much as of the method, so a deployment cannot read Table~\ref{tab:real} as evidence that the optimizer will lead on
its own labelling.

