\section{What the Evidence Supports}
One conclusion holds across both workloads: the value of this formulation is not in its solver. On real data a greedy heuristic over the proposed utility returned the same allocation as the exact program at every seed, a paired difference of exactly zero. On the synthetic benchmark at twenty seeds the exact optimizer is no longer beaten anywhere: it is indistinguishable from the fixed rule wherever the budget alone binds while leading it where waiting or admissibility is in play. That is a correction to the five-seed reading this paper previously reported, it does not rescue the solver, because the gain comes from the threshold rule and the admissibility filter, both of which the formulation supplies without any solver at all. The formulation earns its place by identifying which quantities matter (the forecast delay, the value of the evidence already present, the incident metadata) and by supplying the threshold (\ref{eq:threshold}) that turns them into a rule. What does not hold across both workloads is the ranking, which this revision narrows further. On the synthetic benchmark at twenty seeds the fixed rule is indistinguishable from the optimizer wherever the budget alone binds and loses where admissibility or waiting is in play. On real data the optimizer leads the fixed rule in 15 of 21 comparisons on the label this paper chose, 14 of 21 once class imbalance is handled everywhere and 11 of 21 on a label the dataset's annotators defined, where the lead under normal connectivity falls from seven splits of seven to three. Which simple rule wins is therefore a property of the workload, of the class handling and of the labelling, so the rule has to be derived and tested against held-out data from the deployment, labelled the way the deployment labels it. Seven checks follow, all within the simulation scope of this study:
\begin{itemize}
\item Derive the candidates (a byte-profile fixed rule, the constant threshold (\ref{eq:tstar}) and a greedy ranking with a summary floor where a shared budget binds) and choose among them on held-out events; keep the exact optimizer only if it beats all three.
\item Before optimizing a value function, check what it prices at zero; the tight-budget failure was a misspecification that exact optimization exploited.
\item Treat metadata admissibility as protection against accident, not adversaries; its value falls linearly with forging capability.
\item Recalibrate conformal thresholds per operating condition and across the event boundary the deployment will cross and size the review queue from reviewer capacity.
\item Check whether a shared-budget method still helps when controllers cannot coordinate; the exact knapsack lost far more than a greedy ranking.
\item Add a held-out estimate of predictive contribution to the value function; no existing term detects a modality that carries no signal for the label. Handle class imbalance in every per-modality model before reading the comparison: doing so left the direction of the real-data result intact while moving one rotation's gap from $-17.2$ to $-112.6$ errors and reversing which methods benefit.
\item Score the comparison on more than one labelling of the same records. The result here held across seven event splits and did not hold across a change of label, so an event rotation alone does not establish that a ranking will transfer.
\end{itemize}
The value of the wait-or-act decision itself rests on two quantities this benchmark assumes, how quickly an early decision loses value and how quickly evidence goes stale, which have to come from emergency-management partners and measured networks.

\section{Limitations and Threats to Validity}
\label{sec:limits}
The verification data are synthetic and well separated, which explains error rates below 1\% for the stronger methods and makes the conformal sets nearly binary. Byte sizes, delay distributions, forecast errors and the rate at which evidence ages are assumptions on which the conclusions about fixed rules and waiting depend; Section~\ref{sec:delay} shows the accuracy result under delay disappearing when evidence does not age. Section~\ref{sec:real} removes a smaller part of this limitation than ``real data'' suggests: the link, budgets, loss model, slot structure, sensor modality and severity stay synthetic or fixed, six of nine conditions and three of eleven methods do not carry over and the arrival delay used to test the wait threshold on that corpus is constructed rather than observed. The text pathway's majority-class outcome is no longer unresolved: a twenty-five-cell diagnostic attributes it to class imbalance rather than to the dimensionality of the reduction, with no tested representation clearing a bar fixed in advance in more than two of seven splits.

The link model is the sharpest limitation for an edge-systems audience. Loss, retransmission and replayed measured capacity narrow it, but there is no protocol stack: no MAC layer, routing, contention between nodes, congestion control or bidirectional traffic. There is no hardware testbed and no energy measurement; Section~\ref{sec:compute} reports wall-clock time and peak allocation on one workstation and we decline to infer joules from them. Section~\ref{sec:coord} relaxes the single-controller assumption by static partitioning only. Every statement in this paper about edge deployment is therefore bounded by simulation on a workstation.

Policy coefficients and thresholds were set by hand; the latency penalty is accompanied by a sweep and the main protocol was not pre-registered. The admissibility test favours the filter by construction. The contested-budget difficulty was chosen by a rule fixed in advance, but the budget grid and the competitor were chosen by the authors and both protocols are timestamped only within the package. The severity estimator is weak, so severity-dependent behaviour is barely exercised. Twenty seeds and four test events per seed still limit statistical resolution: three of the eight paired error comparisons include zero, so we treat those three as inconclusive rather than as equivalence. Quadrupling the seeds reversed no reported direction while changing one nominal sign, the low-bandwidth comparison, against this paper's own earlier reading. On the real-data side the seeds vary model fitting only, which is why we rotate the event split; the seven assignments reuse the same events and are not independent. Social-media and sensor sources carry participation and placement bias. Improved disaster outcomes cannot be claimed without measured network traces, more disasters and prospective studies with emergency-management partners.


Two tests the reviewers asked for could not be completed on the available inputs and we state the grounds rather than substitute something weaker without saying so. Reproducing the published CrisisMMD baselines on the official split needs three things this machine does not hold: the official train, development and test split, which the corpus readme names as a separate archive that was not retrieved; the published baseline values themselves, which are not retained here, so a reproduction gap has no verified reference to be measured against; and the image files, of which only derived embeddings remain, so no baseline can be computed from pixels. The established tasks also cannot be run at their published label cardinality, two classes for informative against not informative and eight for the humanitarian categories, because the decision machinery in the reference implementation is fixed at three and that implementation is carried unchanged so its published numbers stay checkable. The three-category restriction of Section~\ref{sec:reallabel} is what those constraints permit; it is not the published task and no reproduction gap is reported. An emulator run was specified as optional and was not executed because the link-shell tooling is not installed here, so nothing in this paper rests on packet emulation.
\section{Reproducibility, Oversight and Data Availability}
The package contains the source, an environment lock, the configuration, event split identifiers, calibration temperatures and thresholds, the derived wait thresholds, seeds, integrity tests, record-level predictions for every method and condition, provenance envelopes, the two frozen contested-budget protocols with their SHA-256 digests and scripts that regenerate every table and figure. On one CPU architecture reruns reproduce every output file byte for byte across Python 3.12 to 3.14. Across architectures about 10\% of source observation vectors differ in their last bits because the generator's Beta and Dirichlet draws reach platform libm; every reported quantity is written at six significant figures or fewer and is unaffected and the provenance digest is taken over the observation rounded to nine decimals. The real-data pipeline depends on a pretrained encoder and on PyTorch whose versions are not pinned, so byte-for-byte reproduction of the derived records is not claimed; a digest of those records, the encoder identifier, the split, the label mapping and the source URL are recorded instead. The package changelog lists design errors found and corrected during development; results from before those corrections are superseded.

The method is decision support, not an autonomous responder. Reviewers receive the evidence set, the uncertainty, the conflict indicators, the reason codes and the status rule that fired and the interface must distinguish observed evidence from model-derived fields. Governance follows the NIST AI RMF lifecycle~\cite{nistrmf}; deployment requires agency authority, privacy and cybersecurity review, periodic recalibration (Section~\ref{sec:coverage}) and a staffing plan for the queue of Section~\ref{sec:queue}. Raw transmission must be policy-gated.

The package is maintained at \url{https://github.com/bdata-arindam/Edge}. The version behind every result reported here is 2.6.17, whose \texttt{SHA256SUMS} lists 113 file digests; 112 match the copy accompanying this submission, the exception being the citation metadata file, whose author fields stay placeholders until the release is deposited. We quote no archival identifier because the release is not yet deposited, so there is none to cite, nor does any claim in this paper rest on one. Depositing the release under a permanent identifier is outstanding and is the second of the two items the authors must close before camera-ready. The synthetic verification contains no personal data. Section~\ref{sec:real} uses CrisisMMD v2.0, obtained from its authors under their terms and not redistributed; we ship derived features, byte counts and labels, not the posts or images. Operational use of field reports or agency data would require institutional, legal and privacy review.

\section{Conclusion}
We formulated disaster evidence transmission as a shared-budget knapsack across concurrent incidents in which waiting for late evidence is an explicit decision with a delay threshold and admissibility is checked before any evidence is valued or fused. In the synthetic benchmark at twenty seeds the simple rules the formulation generates are indistinguishable from the optimizer it justifies wherever the budget alone binds, a pre-registered test found the optimizer worse than its pre-specified competitor at the tightest budget while one of its five differences survived adjustment in the solver's favour, by one error in 720, the admissibility filter's protection falls to nothing in proportion to an adversary's ability to forge metadata and the conformal guarantee does not transfer across conditions. On real data the optimizer leads the fixed rule in 15 of 21 comparisons across seven event splits on the damage label this paper chose, in 14 of 21 once class imbalance is handled in every per-modality model and in 11 of 21 on a label the dataset's annotators defined, where its lead under normal connectivity falls from all seven splits to three. A single-modality rule beats everything on that chosen label, partly because the imbalance was left in place. The conformal guarantee and the byte model both fail against measured data. The text pathway is now diagnosed: its majority-class behaviour is a class-imbalance effect rather than an artefact of the reduction, no tested representation clears a bar fixed in advance and the value function detects none of it. Transferred to real data the delay threshold keeps a 72-fold tail-latency reduction in every split while costing accuracy rather than saving it, because that corpus does not decay evidence while a decision waits. What survives both workloads is that the formulation earns its place by naming the quantities that matter and supplying the threshold that turns them into a rule, never by the solver, which did not reliably beat a greedy alternative. Which simple rule to deploy has to be derived against the data a system will see and scored on the labelling that system will use. Field value under a real protocol stack and on edge hardware remains to be established.

